WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.143
Back in the storeroom.

2
00:00:04.143 --> 00:00:10.442
Last lesson, every box got a label with a blank: Box of, and then whatever the caller writes in.

3
00:00:10.442 --> 00:00:11.820
Any caller, any type.

4
00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.100
That freedom has a price, and it surprises people.

5
00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:18.961
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

6
00:00:18.961 --> 00:00:21.380
I only ever pass fruit to my function.

7
00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.690
So why can't my generic code ask a T for its weight?

8
00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.855
Can't the compiler see my callers?

9
00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:31.017
Start with a plain T, with no sign on it.

10
00:00:31.017 --> 00:00:35.606
describe takes any T, and prints its value and its hash code.

11
00:00:35.606 --> 00:00:37.937
Gala, 180, even null: all fine.

12
00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:43.075
That works because toString, hashCode and equals exist on every type.

13
00:00:43.075 --> 00:00:47.913
In Kotlin, the type that covers every value, null included, is Any?.

14
00:00:47.913 --> 00:00:49.763
A plain T can do what Any?

15
00:00:49.763 --> 00:00:51.541
can do, and nothing more.

16
00:00:52.467 --> 00:00:54.912
Now ask for something more specific.

17
00:00:54.912 --> 00:00:56.950
lengthOf returns x dot length.

18
00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:00.075
Every caller you have in mind passes a String.

19
00:01:00.075 --> 00:01:01.162
Does it compile?

20
00:01:01.162 --> 00:01:02.249
Pause and guess.

21
00:01:05.233 --> 00:01:07.364
No: unresolved reference, length.

22
00:01:07.364 --> 00:01:09.366
The compiler isn't being fussy.

23
00:01:09.366 --> 00:01:13.370
Someone could call lengthOf with 42, and an Int has no length.

24
00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:20.151
A generic function must work for every type a caller may pick, so it can only use what all of them share.

25
00:01:21.067 --> 00:01:23.914
Comparing fails too, with a stranger message.

26
00:01:23.914 --> 00:01:28.850
Write a greater than b on two T's, and Kotlin talks about compareTo on String.

27
00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:30.559
You never mentioned String.

28
00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:36.693
It's like asking the clerk for a ripe apple, and hearing that the paper bags are the wrong shape.

29
00:01:36.693 --> 00:01:37.671
A roundabout no.

30
00:01:37.671 --> 00:01:42.925
So when an error names some other type's method, read it as: this isn't defined for T.

31
00:01:43.833 --> 00:01:45.232
Back to the storeroom.

32
00:01:45.232 --> 00:01:47.457
One box carries a sign: fruit only.

33
00:01:47.457 --> 00:01:50.509
The sign shuts out the hammer and the till roll.

34
00:01:50.509 --> 00:01:59.028
In return, whoever unpacks the box knows something about every item in it: each one has a weight, so it can go straight on the scales.

35
00:01:59.233 --> 00:02:00.117
Let's map it.

36
00:02:00.117 --> 00:02:05.761
The sign is a bound on T, written T : Fruit in Kotlin, and T extends Fruit in Java.

37
00:02:05.761 --> 00:02:09.501
The hammer at the door is a caller the bound shuts out.

38
00:02:09.501 --> 00:02:14.193
And the scales are the function's body, which may now use the weight.

39
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.154
Here's the shop's fruit in code.

40
00:02:17.154 --> 00:02:20.749
Fruit is an interface with a name and a weight in grams.

41
00:02:20.749 --> 00:02:23.766
Apple is a small data class that implements it.

42
00:02:23.766 --> 00:02:28.645
heavier takes two T's, where T must be a Fruit, and returns the heavier one.

43
00:02:28.645 --> 00:02:33.267
Inside, a dot grams compiles, because every T is now some kind of Fruit.

44
00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:37.468
Two apples: Gala at 180 grams, and Fuji at 150.

45
00:02:37.468 --> 00:02:40.170
The demo prints Gala, 180 grams.

46
00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:41.521
And two strings?

47
00:02:41.521 --> 00:02:47.431
Argument type mismatch: actual type is String, but Fruit was expected.

48
00:02:47.431 --> 00:02:50.301
The sign did its job, at the door.

49
00:02:51.233 --> 00:02:53.596
You might be thinking: why a T at all?

50
00:02:53.596 --> 00:02:56.767
Just take two Fruit parameters, and return a Fruit.

51
00:02:56.767 --> 00:02:59.253
Fewer angle brackets, same thing, right?

52
00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:01.085
Let's be fair, and try it.

53
00:03:01.085 --> 00:03:03.574
Two apples go in, and a Fruit comes out.

54
00:03:03.574 --> 00:03:08.801
To get your Apple back, you'd need a cast: the very thing the last lesson took away.

55
00:03:08.801 --> 00:03:11.291
With T, two apples in give an Apple out.

56
00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.350
The most common bound isn't a class you wrote.

57
00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:16.445
It's Comparable.

58
00:03:16.445 --> 00:03:22.196
Read T : Comparable&lt;T&gt; aloud as: T is something that can be compared with another T.

59
00:03:22.196 --> 00:03:26.989
Now a greater than b compiles, because the bound promises a compareTo.

60
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:29.388
So 180 and 150 give 180.

61
00:03:29.388 --> 00:03:35.768
And Gala and Fuji give Gala, the one that comes later in the alphabet.

62
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.262
Now hand maxOf2 two apples.

63
00:03:38.262 --> 00:03:39.351
What happens?

64
00:03:39.351 --> 00:03:40.692
Pause and guess.

65
00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:48.798
It fails: actual type is Apple, but a Comparable was expected.

66
00:03:48.798 --> 00:03:50.771
Apple has no natural order.

67
00:03:50.771 --> 00:03:53.402
Should two apples compare by weight?

68
00:03:53.402 --> 00:03:53.987
By name?

69
00:03:53.987 --> 00:03:54.644
By price?

70
00:03:54.644 --> 00:03:58.664
The compiler refuses to pick favorites, and it's right.

71
00:03:59.567 --> 00:04:00.478
So you choose.

72
00:04:00.478 --> 00:04:03.669
Make Apple a Comparable of Apple, by weight, say.

73
00:04:03.669 --> 00:04:11.875
Or, usually better, keep Apple plain, and pass a Comparator, a small object that compares two values, where you need an order.

74
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.051
The sign picture breaks in one place.

75
00:04:15.051 --> 00:04:19.250
A real fruit only sign is checked by a person, who looks at the item.

76
00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:22.170
The compiler only trusts what the types declare.

77
00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:27.099
A class with a grams property that doesn't say it's a Fruit is still turned away.

78
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:28.855
Now the name.

79
00:04:28.855 --> 00:04:34.311
A sign like this is called an upper bound: T must be this type, or a subtype of it.

80
00:04:34.311 --> 00:04:40.227
For now, read subtype as a class that inherits from it, the way Apple inherits from Fruit.

81
00:04:40.227 --> 00:04:42.725
The next lesson makes that word exact.

82
00:04:43.633 --> 00:04:44.822
And the guardrail.

83
00:04:44.822 --> 00:04:48.652
A bound shuts some callers out, and lets the body do more.

84
00:04:48.652 --> 00:04:51.161
So ask for exactly what the body uses.

85
00:04:51.161 --> 00:04:54.463
Every extra demand turns callers away for nothing.

86
00:04:54.463 --> 00:05:01.331
Later in this lesson, a where clause asks for two things at once, and T : Any keeps null out of the box.

87
00:05:02.233 --> 00:05:04.877
So, why can't a plain T tell you its weight?

88
00:05:04.877 --> 00:05:07.701
Because the compiler doesn't know your callers.

89
00:05:07.701 --> 00:05:09.143
It only knows the label.

90
00:05:09.143 --> 00:05:13.289
Put a sign on the label, and the body may use what the sign promises.

91
00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:14.466
Quick check.

92
00:05:14.466 --> 00:05:16.880
maxOf2 of 1, and the string a.

93
00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:18.490
Both are comparable.

94
00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:19.777
Does it compile?

95
00:05:22.967 --> 00:05:23.197
No.

96
00:05:23.197 --> 00:05:29.956
One T can't be an Int and a String at once: actual type is String, but Int was expected.
